# Secrets shared across every destination. Kamal reads this file ALWAYS -- it is
# the one filename in the list that does not vary with `-d`
# (kamal/secrets.rb:46):
#
#   destination (none)       -> .kamal/secrets-common, .kamal/secrets
#   destination production   -> .kamal/secrets-common, .kamal/secrets.production
#
# That asymmetry is the trap this file exists to solve. Anything put in
# .kamal/secrets is read ONLY when no destination is given: `kamal deploy -d
# production` skips it completely and fails on a missing key even though the value
# is sitting in the repository. Values that every destination needs belong HERE.
#
# THE RULE: no line in this file may contain a literal credential. Every value is
# either a `$(...)` command substitution that fetches the real value from
# somewhere, or it does not belong here. That single invariant is what makes the
# file safe to commit -- Kamal parses it with plain dotenv semantics and performs no
# encryption, so a literal password here would be a password in the repository.
# This is the "DO NOT ENTER RAW CREDENTIALS HERE!" warning in Kamal's own template.
#
# Per-destination overrides live in .kamal/secrets.<destination>, which merge over
# this file. The RDS password and the infrastructure values are in
# .kamal/secrets.production, gitignored.

# Rails signing key and JWT signing key. Both live in AWS Secrets Manager under
# the `salary-management/prod` secret, as a single JSON object. Each line reads the
# secret and pulls out its own key, so the two can rotate independently in Secrets
# Manager without this file changing.
SECRET_KEY_BASE=$(aws secretsmanager get-secret-value --secret-id salary-management/prod --region us-east-1 --query SecretString --output text | python3 -c "import sys,json; print(json.load(sys.stdin)['SECRET_KEY_BASE'])")
JWT_SECRET=$(aws secretsmanager get-secret-value --secret-id salary-management/prod --region us-east-1 --query SecretString --output text | python3 -c "import sys,json; print(json.load(sys.stdin)['JWT_SECRET'])")

# ECR password for registry.login. This cannot come from Secrets Manager: it is a
# short-lived token from `aws ecr get-login-password` that AWS issues per call and
# expires in 12 hours, so there is nothing durable to store. Fetching it fresh on
# every deploy is the correct behaviour, not a workaround.
KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD=$(aws ecr get-login-password --region us-east-1)
